• The limitation was the REPL’s local variable visibility cap, not the names themselves.

  Inside the loop body, the evaluator needs all visible variables:

  i
  angle
  ripplephase
  ripplewidth
  rippledelta
  rippleamp
  samples
  ringx
  ringy

  That is 9 visible variables: the loop variable i plus the 8 declared locals. The REPL’s local expression variable buffer is capped at MAX_EXPR_VARS, which appears to be 8 in this code path. When the example
  was exported to C and imported back, the import/normalization path only preserved the first 8 visible variables, so ringy fell out of scope.

  That made this line fail during import:

  glVertex3f(ringx, ringy, 0);

  The importer could still see ringx, but not ringy, so it warned that it could not parse the line and dropped part of the example. That caused the example round-trip test to fail.

  So the fix was to reduce the number of simultaneously visible variables. I removed angle and samples by inlining their expressions and kept the more meaningful ripple names:

  ripplephase
  ripplewidth
  rippledelta
  rippleamp
  x
  y

  Inside the loop that gives 7 visible variables including i, which stays under the cap and round-trips cleanly.

